WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.310
From 1997 to 2009, the Fox animated series
King of the Hill was among the most consistently

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.910
funny, smart, and sharply satirical shows
on TV.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:15.040
Created by Mike Judge and Greg Daniels, it
took place in the all-American Texas town

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.970
of Arlen, and depicted the lives of propane
salesman Hank Hill…

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.960
"My name is Hank Hill.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.780
And I sell propane and propane accessories."

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.560
...substitute Spanish teacher Peggy Hill…

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.680
...their one-of-a-kind son Bobby…

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.700
"That's my purse! I don't know you!"

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:43.040
...and their neighbors and friends.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.380
Here are some things you might not have known
about King of the Hill.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.629
Welcome to Arlen

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:52.210
After the success of Beavis &amp; Butt-head at
MTV, Fox asked Mike Judge to a develop an

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.660
animated series the network could pair with
The Simpsons.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.180
Judge, who had grown up in the southwest,
produced a very rough idea of a Texas-set

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.180
series.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.559
Fox executives suggested he find a seasoned
comedy writer to help him flesh out a script,

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:08.940
so he got together with The Simpsons' Greg
Daniels, taking him on a trip to Texas to

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.000
give him the feel for the show.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.330
Daniels added a lot of his own material to
Judge's original concept, including the characters

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.240
of Luanne and Cotton Hill.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.030
"C'mon grease monkey, let's tangle!"

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.880
He also added the notion that Dale was a conspiracy
theorist whose wife was cheating on him.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.659
Then, instead of a pilot episode, Judge and
Daniels devised a "pencil test."

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:33.060
In the two minute video, Hank Hill, voiced
by Judge, pitched Fox executives on making

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.340
a show about his life.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:38.440
"I got some ideas for advertisers if you put
us on the air.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:43.180
Uhh, trucks… pork rinds… we have a lot
of those in our house."

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.040
What's up with Boomhauer?

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.400
"Yeah, man, I tell you what, I seen the dang
ol' rake go right down miss…

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.700
Smithsonian, man.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.250
Right next to Lincoln's have them arch chair,
man, dang ol' caveman section."

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:58.409
Throughout almost every episode of King of
the Hill, the character of Boomhauer remains

00:01:58.409 --> 00:01:59.409
a mystery.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:03.530
Although he's one of Hank's core group of
beer buddies and appears in most every episode,

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:04.880
little is known about him.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.890
The fact that his first name is Jeff and he's
a Texas Ranger isn't even revealed until the

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:09.890
series finale.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.150
He's also impossible to understand because
he speaks in a rapid-fire Texas drawl.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.640
"Tell you what, man, I'm gonna take that down
to the DMV, man, and take my picture, I had

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:18.910
a hair hootie that day, man.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.360
Been four dang ol' years been going looking
like Cokie Roberts, man."

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.940
Mike Judge voiced the character, and as he
mentioned on an episode of Jimmy Kimmel Live,

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.950
he based his technique on an irate TV viewer
who once left him an incomprehensible voicemail

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.220
to complain about Judge's previous show, Beavis
&amp; Butt-Head.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.900
"The message started out, he goes, "I been
y'all for better month now, write y'all every

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.810
time that dang ol' pokies butt ol', come on."

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:44.250
The tale of Dale

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.160
The role of chain-smoking conspiracy theorist
and exterminator Dale Gribble was almost played

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:54.240
by Daniel Stern, the Home Alone star and The
Wonder Years narrator, but he reportedly wanted

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.640
too much money, and producers sent him away.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.640
"Ahh!"

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:04.120
He would have joined a voice cast that was
populated by well-known actors and experienced

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:05.440
vocal talent.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:10.380
"Prepare to be destroyed by the deadliest
of all martial arts…

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.760
MONKEY STYLE!"

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.940
Around the same time, King of the Hill co-creator
Greg Daniels took in a comedy showcase at

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:22.780
a Los Angeles club where a Texas-born comic
named Johnny Hardwick told some stories about

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.420
his family and upbringing.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.390
After his set, Daniels offered him a job as
a writer on King of the Hill.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:32.120
Once in the fold, Hardwick auditioned for
Dale, telling the Austin Chronicle that he

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.819
took inspiration for his characterization
from counter-culture icon William S. Burroughs

00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:40.940
and Matthew McConaughey in Dazed and Confused…
along with a little bit of Jack Nicholson.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:44.910
Hardwick got the part, of course — his first-ever
acting gig.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.040
Where is Arlen?

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:51.959
The Hill family lives in Arlen, a sleepy medium-sized
city in Texas about 100 miles from Dallas.

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:53.080
Or is it?

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.930
Like Springfield or Shelbyville on The Simpsons,
Arlen isn't a real place.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:01.970
The town's name is reminiscent of Garland,
a suburb of Dallas where Mike Judge once lived.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:06.629
But while TV's Arlen isn't part of a fictional
Dallas-Fort Worth "metroplex," Judge says

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:10.290
the town was really inspired by Richardson,
a different Dallas suburb.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.290
"Dallas?

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:13.790
I don't want you going to Dallas at all!

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:17.410
That place is crawling with crackheads and
debutantes.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:19.220
And half of them play for the Cowboys!"

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:24.050
Despite the show's general adherence to continuity
and canon, Arlen shifts to wherever the story

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:25.250
needs it to go.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:30.060
Just like Durndle and McMaynerberry, Arlen
is nowhere and everywhere at once… as long

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.930
as that everywhere is in Texas.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:33.610
The unfinished spinoff

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:38.030
King of the Hill was a long-running success
for Fox with a large universe of characters,

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:42.910
so it's not surprising that in the early 2000s,
Fox ordered a pilot for a live-action spinoff

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.110
called Monsignor Martinez.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:48.270
"Vaya con dios."

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:52.810
Written by King of the Hill's Jim Dauterive
and directed by Mike Judge, it starred Venezuelan

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:57.840
actor Ivo Cutzarida as a trigger-happy avenging
priest at the center of a Spanish-language

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.870
soap opera popular in Arlen.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:03.840
Unfortunately, while the show had a complete
pilot produced, it was never aired.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.910
And while it's rumored to have shown up online
long ago, it's considered to be long-lost

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:09.570
media today.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:10.650
You got Lucky

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.210
One of the biggest additions to the later
seasons of King of the Hill was Elroy "Lucky"

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.600
Kleinschmidt, Luanne's sketchy boyfriend and
later husband.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:22.720
His nickname comes from his main source of
income: a cash settlement he won because he

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.009
slipped on a puddle of urine in a Costco.

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:27.890
"And don't you know, I slipped on pee pee
and broke two vertebrae which had to be fused

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:28.560
together."

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:37.610
Lucky was voiced by the late rock legend Tom
Petty — and he kind of looked like Tom Petty,

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.750
too, which is totally by design.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.870
When describing the character to Mike Judge,
writer John Altschuler said Lucky ought to

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.440
look like "Tom Petty without the success."

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.590
So the show's producers actually pursued Petty
for the role.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:54.350
The rock star, who had very little acting
experience outside his music videos, enthusiastically

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:55.100
signed up.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.000
Back from the dead

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.950
Fox famously canceled Family Guy in 2002 and
then brought it back in 2005, but most people

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:09.270
don't know that the network did the same thing
with King of the Hill.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:14.960
The show's 11th season finale, "Lucky's Wedding
Suit," aired in May 2007 and was intended

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.900
to serve as a conclusion to the series.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.259
It kind of had to be the last episode of King
of the Hill, too, because Fox had canceled

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:22.259
the show.

00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:23.520
Judge told the Chicago Tribune,

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.370
"And I mean completely canceled, as in everyone
moved out and cleaned out their desks."

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:32.880
Imagine their surprise when Fox ordered more
episodes, and King of the Hill ended up running

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.800
for another two years.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.120
The return of Hank

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:41.870
King of the Hill rode the proverbial lawnmower
into the Texas twilight in September 2009.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:44.520
But on TV these days, everything old is new
again.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:50.390
At the 2017 Television Critics Association
summer press tour, Fox CEO and chairperson

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:55.169
Dana Walden said "preliminary conversations"
have taken place between the network and King

00:06:55.169 --> 00:06:58.180
of the Hill creators Mike Judge and Greg Daniels.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:02.920
Walden thinks Hank, Peggy, and the rest would
"have a point of view" about America today,

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:04.819
and she wants to explore a revival.

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:07.319
Sounds like a pretty dang ol' great idea to
us.

00:07:07.319 --> 00:07:08.810
Thanks for watching!

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.770
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:14.140
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

